      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62455,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumours are typically large, unilateral, solid masses with a greyish-pink or yellow to pale-brown cut surface.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:59:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62456,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Microscopically, these tumours show a variable and haphazard admixture of germ cells and sex cord cells. The germ cells are found singly or in small clusters and are large, with pleomorphic nuclei and ample, often clear and PAS-positive cytoplasm, resembling dysgerminoma tumour cells. Sex cord cells may form cords or trabeculae, hollow or solid tubules (sometimes resembling sex cord tumours with annular tubules), or cysts, or they may grow diffusely. Immunohistochemically, the sex cord elements are typically positive for inhibin. The germ cells resemble dysgerminoma cells and are immunoreactive for PLAP, OCT4, and KIT {16538443}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:59:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62457,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:59:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62458,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:59:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62459,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> an ovarian tumour consisting of an irregular admixture of dysgerminoma-like germ cells and sex cord cells not exhibiting the distinctive appearance of a gonadoblastoma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:59:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62460,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "<p>This entity may be staged according to the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM classification (see <em>TNM staging of ovarian, fallopian tube, and primary peritoneal carcinoma</em>&nbsp;[[Brierley JD, Gospodarowicz MK, Wittekind C, editors. TNM classification of malignant tumours. 8th ed. Oxford (UK): Wiley-Blackwell; 2017.]]) and the FIGO staging system.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-15T11:42:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62461,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 103,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most of the lesions are clinically benign, and the development of malignant germ cell tumours and metastasis is rare. Dysgerminoma or another malignant germ cell tumour develops in about 10% of all patients, more frequently in postpubertal patients {17581418}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:59:47"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]